		"`examples/addons/car-paint/` makes the flakes with Deliot and Belcour's glint model: `paint.wgsl` counts the facets in the pixel footprint that reflect the main light, from each flake cell's own random value and drawn from the Poisson form of the binomial, through the lobe hook. The first model drew no glint: across the six paint references no pixel was brighter with the flakes than without them, because a light of 0.02 radians, 2.3 degrees across, left about one flake in 1,400 to reflect, so the paint only lost its smooth highlight. The default is now 0.25 radians, 28.6 degrees across, and the test body's flake cells cover equal distances, so a glint draws as a 2 by 2 pixel dot. `tests/image/car-paint.spec.ts` measures the flakes at a mean channel difference of 0.00262 to 0.00267, with 0.01607 to 0.01631 of the channels changing by more than 8 steps and 216 to 232 pixels brighter, against floors of 0.0013, 0.008 and 108; its glint count is the only guard against a lobe that darkens without sparkling, since the mean and share floors pass such a paint. The part test passed on the Mac's GPU and on SwiftShader, and the nine paint pictures of each set were redrawn, the software set from CI run 40192893324. `crates/null3d-shaders/tests/material.rs` builds the shipped WGSL on both paths; D-255",
